#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/id #uuid "5a52cc3b-4e4b-5b66-b1e0-589e3dd806e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-4219" :question/problem "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=2}^\\infty\\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)^n.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\displaystyle \\sum ar^n.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is $a_2,$ and so the sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_2}{1-r}, \\qquad |r| <        1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=\\frac{8}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is:\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_3}{a_2} = \\dfrac{\\left( \\dfrac{8}{27}\\right)}{\\left( \\dfrac{4}{9}\\right)} = \\dfrac 2 3\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{9})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{4}{9})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "90ac2db3-26c1-533d-8a0d-edceba8d565f" :question/math-academy-id "e-685" :question/problem "Evaluate the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty 2(0.5)^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\displaystyle \\sum ar^n.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad |r| <    1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\na_1 = 2(0.5)^{1} = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_2}{a_1} = \\dfrac{2(0.5)^2}{2(0.5)^1} = 0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(0.5)^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "6126f9d5-f163-5167-b524-710146791e06" :question/math-academy-id "e-686" :question/problem "Given that $a_1=-2$ and $\\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}} = -0.4$ for all integers $n \\geq 1,$ find the value of $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_1}{1-r}, \\qquad |r| <    1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1=-2.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_{n}} = -0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1-(-0.4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1.4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "fff2b9cd-5963-501d-b50a-848850e88a2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-687" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series $\\displaystyle \\sum_{n=3}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3.$ In this case, the first term is $a_3,$ and so the sum to infinity $S_\\infty$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\nS_\\infty = \\dfrac{a_3}{1-r}, \\qquad |r| <        1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\na_3 = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2(3)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{6} = 5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{7}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\na_4 = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2(4)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{8} = 5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac{a_4}{a_3} = \\dfrac{5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{9}}{5\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{7}} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{7}}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5}{128})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{128})(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3(32)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252d7399-6586-5f84-8030-45ae317c5358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528396} {:answer/id #uuid "58001f98-cc6b-50fc-8515-2b430ddb064e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528508} {:answer/id #uuid "9847580b-2e31-55ab-b2c1-6d446fe03134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528661} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd34d4d-2d1d-5258-8b9f-3e59fda9493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528674} {:answer/id #uuid "a9586b5e-6b6a-5eb0-8d9c-fb74207d48ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "252d7399-6586-5f84-8030-45ae317c5358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5299a706-5fb9-52db-936b-bcf34934c585" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5cec570-331b-5761-a994-262c8147d70e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124575" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=10$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1-(-0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1+0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{1.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,000}{125} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14bf80a3-1665-5977-9753-8899ce180734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528352} {:answer/id #uuid "631ca0b5-ae05-5843-8fce-eaa43004c3a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528534} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbbebd6-1052-5e30-bf33-b4a804d2efb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528644} {:answer/id #uuid "e9c2927b-5f83-5559-9be7-b5aabb0973ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528878} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0d9032-620e-5573-8ccc-ca1cd595057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0d9032-620e-5573-8ccc-ca1cd595057d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528923} :answer-field/id #uuid "3091e31c-4430-5ffe-b4b7-0a02be1e4edf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf1f4594-bcb0-53bd-9558-7de1afb93bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124577" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-14$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-14.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-14\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070e71d3-4fa0-5f24-95c6-4392df98055f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528321} {:answer/id #uuid "358ce659-62c7-5b50-8b6a-72ee4e8e55df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528435} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec18b62-e688-5729-8acb-74067dce2afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528461} {:answer/id #uuid "b575e07e-689b-50db-9fe4-49b41ddcdf6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528744} {:answer/id #uuid "e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e072663c-1824-5e1b-a41f-e8be263083ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528848} :answer-field/id #uuid "160bbd50-69e6-5813-9152-8fb571501011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df45f3a3-f545-5c42-98d5-edf671e88e36" :question/math-academy-id "q-124584" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }10{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad32293-81c4-5632-9b56-dce7d9c78668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528328} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528453} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4e7ff3-16b6-51f5-a98c-6ce0c3972663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528518} {:answer/id #uuid "843d0574-e70b-5e22-b58c-6a661198e3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528614} {:answer/id #uuid "b2433a72-ac85-5f1f-b794-39e122d2d8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb6d77-f65e-5ff5-becb-dd2f3d35c069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528453} :answer-field/id #uuid "5101b7ce-f6ad-523b-93e4-83e6665f500b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd479934-e321-5035-bf71-51bd7984a9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-124588" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=5 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}=\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{6})}{5}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }180{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a73c4b15-30e3-596c-8756-b257e8707bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089412} {:answer/id #uuid "aedd6e37-5993-58b3-a481-c0ddee6b3a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089413} {:answer/id #uuid "f3690cdb-243d-5845-a43d-208bfd74168b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089414} {:answer/id #uuid "0d097fd3-bab5-5639-a0b4-2c8c444dcda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089415} {:answer/id #uuid "645b6acc-489a-5763-a396-c2d45c7bec62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aedd6e37-5993-58b3-a481-c0ddee6b3a98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089413} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb38ad5b-4ed2-5c45-954e-108522d434b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7322fdcb-2ecd-5661-80da-ace9d4087c58" :question/math-academy-id "q-13580" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.5)^{4n}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3(0.5)^{4(1)}=3(0.5)^{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=3(0.5)^{4(2)}=3(0.5)^{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3(0.5)^{8}}{3(0.5)^{4}}=(0.5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3(0.5)^{4n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(0.5)^{4}}{1-(0.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fb69b0-fb4a-5df6-aac8-69f15b52ee53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fb69b0-fb4a-5df6-aac8-69f15b52ee53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528309} :answer-field/id #uuid "92506140-a021-54d4-9afc-c6047e832c6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac1cfe40-911c-5173-ab2c-22ea711c204a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350577" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=5$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.2$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=5.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1-(-0.2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(1+0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc33e925-2bf6-51a9-b4d5-52ab4d837e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528929} :answer-field/id #uuid "77d5f4a7-be7e-5df2-a989-37fce15016eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c0c2f91-9457-576f-ab5b-77c8f4a530ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-350579" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}=\\frac{12}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}{4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{12}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df9bedc8-89b3-5ab1-b0b3-a9b2ac3b4951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528847} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13a95c4-8252-5e9e-aaed-c7750144cf3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56e0e8e1-0cfd-5d25-b2ed-327a18d60529" :question/math-academy-id "q-350582" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}=3.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-\\frac{3}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(\\frac{5}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "976b5925-40fb-5125-b552-7a9d7cec7fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528659} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cde64eb-fc8f-574e-a4b3-4a914b9a4d5e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f15411-79a5-546c-8ee4-965cb1e88cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-350607" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $4.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{4},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}=\\frac{16}{81} \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{5}=\\frac{32}{243}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{4}}=\\frac{(\\frac{32}{243})}{(\\frac{16}{81})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=4}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{81})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1af309f6-8280-5978-9ceb-4d701d91b214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528374} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b93b28c-ccc2-5022-a0b7-15dc266d4717" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a6fcb51-44e7-59f8-9078-8afece3073a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-350624" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6(0.5)^{2(1)}=6(0.5)^{2}=6(0.25)=1.5,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=6(0.5)^{2(2)}=6(0.5)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6(0.5)^{4}}{6(0.5)^{2}}=(0.5)^{2}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(0.5)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{1-0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{0.75} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea5f493-396d-519f-9baa-177ed893c00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528807} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c0bbe3-e9a9-52f6-ab2f-3984c5548c8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db8cfdc-3e22-5283-b109-341d9daf5b09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350625" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(1)}=(-7)(0.3)^{2}=(-7)(0.09)=-0.63,\n$$\n\nand the second term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=(-7)(0.3)^{2(2)}=(-7)(0.3)^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-7)(0.3)^{4}}{(-7)(0.3)^{2}}=(0.3)^{2}=0.09.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-7)(0.3)^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.63)}{1-0.09} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0.63}{0.91} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7282c2a-54bb-5833-928b-ed4ac66fa331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7282c2a-54bb-5833-928b-ed4ac66fa331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232984} :answer-field/id #uuid "d78186cd-42ea-5ba4-ba88-adb813f8e3ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ccdb37f-3fc9-5a29-ba10-0b973b38de6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-350648" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $3,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{3-2}=6(-\\frac{1}{5})=-\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n$$\n{a}_{4}=6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{4-2}=6(\\frac{1}{25})=\\frac{6}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(\\frac{6}{25})}{(-\\frac{6}{5})}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }6{(-\\frac{1}{5})}^{\\,n-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{3}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{6}{5})}{(\\frac{6}{5})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092761 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9984b00a-dafa-5ce2-809f-259e529e40d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090767} {:answer/id #uuid "d64c5fde-9796-599e-bfc7-dd3bf4d8b9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090768} {:answer/id #uuid "1f19ab89-a3d1-5650-91e2-ecb1d0b83c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{180}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090769} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9b93e6-6c4e-59cc-916a-c4ff3ca8b6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090770} {:answer/id #uuid "6a01f4cc-d02e-5522-be0a-92c3148d3b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f19ab89-a3d1-5650-91e2-ecb1d0b83c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{180}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090769} :answer-field/id #uuid "21429259-d19b-5015-9e9b-1799bacbedec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ae9971f-bfdd-59a5-a032-19bc1cd6f90a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50484" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-18$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.9$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-18.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-0.9.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-18}{1-(-0.9)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{1+0.9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{1.9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{180}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaba35a4-5f6b-578c-9fbb-90b041c19803" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Given Its First Term and Common Ratio"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb127fe-81a1-51d8-a7f3-7f6901827eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528375} {:answer/id #uuid "3574038e-055d-56a6-9f8a-f1deedf83863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528434} {:answer/id #uuid "359b2829-aef3-5244-a711-6b233756f5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{23}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528436} {:answer/id #uuid "75aed8b7-b806-509d-904b-d5e5a1544691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528572} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bcd84-ba3a-519d-a0e6-d8c907296ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{27}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75aed8b7-b806-509d-904b-d5e5a1544691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528572} :answer-field/id #uuid "4409dd34-9ab8-5a98-a1d2-9602397921b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "14f76056-5e3c-539d-8f82-855878eb07d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-50498" :question/problem "Given that ${a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}$ for all integers $n\\ge 1,$ find the value of ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is geometric series because the ratio of any two consecutive terms is constant.\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nWe're told that the first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=-\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(1-(-\\frac{1}{10}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{5}{2}}{(\\frac{11}{10})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{10}{11} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092256 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04935b22-0a1e-541f-a73a-987f30ee4b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528311} {:answer/id #uuid "1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528368} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbb7959-4b4b-548e-9199-2fcffd9bfefb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528414} {:answer/id #uuid "aaca8c15-d6ca-5c02-bf7f-dcb675eef426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528716} {:answer/id #uuid "f008eb8a-552e-5329-9ff3-06c4e1005ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a25cb22-7e63-590c-9899-23cbef8fefce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528368} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aaf7a48-2de5-5bcf-abf9-e9ddfe97ac48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "499dc119-15d3-5a33-9631-8bc1b2a65d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-5362" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n}.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(1)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2(2)}=\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}}{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}={(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}{(-\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}}{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{7}{10}(\\frac{4}{25})}{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}\\cdot \\frac{25}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bcfda2f-60bb-55ea-8f3d-1753cc34e730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090941} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a86086-4be9-53cf-b46a-c2c41541bbfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090942} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb2de3-726d-57cb-8d3f-680b85a37b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090943} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ff7b57-865d-55a7-93f8-f394224134b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090944} {:answer/id #uuid "e37ac691-f227-5739-a511-9c7057e83008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e37ac691-f227-5739-a511-9c7057e83008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090945} :answer-field/id #uuid "1143b5e5-5e95-53cf-b402-b17151bf9482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c079c397-06b5-5cab-9028-96b72a74da3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-67228" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{64})}{(\\frac{1}{16})}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{16})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a3480c0-71b3-53a7-8e12-a5f45e9dc984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528369} {:answer/id #uuid "410edf4d-43bb-535f-8576-68374334c4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528467} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0391d6-60ff-509f-9126-d08627def1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528666} {:answer/id #uuid "d808212c-d12b-5613-8018-d3e5a2a5ce89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528839} {:answer/id #uuid "fc743163-b80f-5adb-9a55-ae315a62a76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc743163-b80f-5adb-9a55-ae315a62a76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528930} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c44b61-550f-50e7-828e-641fd371090c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbd76d75-8ee7-5bcd-acb2-1f5ae9aa3787" :question/math-academy-id "q-67301" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}}{8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{1}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(1-\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528365} {:answer/id #uuid "5477a5df-1c13-5533-ac14-d6f0af484e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528504} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4ef9fd-0ecb-5af4-9f89-3aa00d5a46be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528605} {:answer/id #uuid "e9b16bc4-347a-513f-ac2b-06d7b3a9de9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528877} {:answer/id #uuid "edbf054f-cc52-584e-8129-fbfe69dad2a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18efe5ab-4593-5cd8-931e-6b73131d825c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528365} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3c0ce87-8111-5c96-ab4f-9edca6a3f6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e8589d7-77a6-5ea2-bd7d-7f7a24b35382" :question/math-academy-id "q-78588" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{1}}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(0.4)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528330} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2772e5-3a67-5a55-9efd-47b34d382cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528334} {:answer/id #uuid "2105d49c-3838-5c6f-a36c-1a555b26bcd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528392} {:answer/id #uuid "25c78422-62a4-52b1-bc72-b3de2ce6a592" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528398} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9324da-a375-57dd-a2cb-31842855799b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bdef5ee-d3f3-562e-aecc-41aeb9ad117d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528330} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15c1c6b-8e24-5b94-b478-74191ff0b0e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75a391e4-92f0-5306-8519-d804235fa403" :question/math-academy-id "q-78591" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{64}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{64}{125})}{(\\frac{16}{25})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(1-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{25})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b98c8710-49f8-550f-aad3-7b78220bd08f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation With an Arbitrary Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12bfe273-020f-5d83-9faf-6cff62334849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528345} {:answer/id #uuid "2c31661b-2050-5fa7-bf5f-9b3a7ef311ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528411} {:answer/id #uuid "2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528412} {:answer/id #uuid "8559fe88-e67b-5f1b-bf3d-bbf3de7441ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528617} {:answer/id #uuid "94cc8691-117b-5651-83f2-f780d14663d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c61ae29-cae7-5ccc-a372-b73c4206fcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528412} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec27395b-38a3-58fa-99e7-8f30382149a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d588b5a8-8a0c-5e4d-8e17-d8c93ee42445" :question/math-academy-id "q-78592" :question/problem "${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{9}{25})=\\frac{3}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3}=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{27}{125})=\\frac{9}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{9}{125})}{(\\frac{3}{25})}=\\frac{9}{125}\\cdot \\frac{25}{3}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{25})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
